1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,000
Let's get into a failure mode that surprises people.

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:08,000
You switched to structured outputs, so the shape is guaranteed, and you still get an

3
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:12,000
answer that's plausible, well-formed, and wrong.

4
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:15,000
Guaranteed structure is not guaranteed correctness.

5
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:22,000
This lesson covers validating a response after generation and re-prompting when it fails.

6
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:25,000
Let's define the vocabulary, three ideas that carry this lesson.

7
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:28,000
First, response validation.

8
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:33,000
That's programmatically checking a reply against a schema and your own business rules

9
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:35,000
after the model generates it.

10
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:41,000
The key phrase is after generation, and it covers meaning, not just syntax.

11
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:44,000
Second, the semantic check.

12
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:50,000
This confirms the values actually make sense and are complete, not merely that the JSON

13
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:51,000
parsed without throwing.

14
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:55,000
A response can be perfectly formed and still be nonsense for your use case.

15
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:58,000
Third, the correction path.

16
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:04,000
When validation fails, you re-prompt the model with the specific error so it can fix its own output.

17
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:07,000
Validation, semantics, correction.

18
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:10,000
Hold those three, and the rest follows.

19
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:13,000
Here's the shape of a reliable pipeline.

20
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:15,000
You don't treat generation as the finish line.

21
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:18,000
You treat it as one step in a loop.

22
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:21,000
Step one, generate the response.

23
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:26,000
Step two, validate it against the schema first, then against your business rules.

24
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:29,000
Step three, and this is the part people skip.

25
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:35,000
On failure, you re-prompt with the exact error so the model can correct itself.

26
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:36,000
Then you validate again.

27
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:42,000
What you're avoiding is the one-shot check, where you generate once, glance at it, and move on.

28
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:47,000
That's exactly where reliability leaks out, because the first generation isn't always right.

29
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:52,000
And without a correction path, a wrong answer just sails through.

30
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:55,000
Let's separate two checks that often get collapsed into one.

31
00:01:55,000 --> 00:02:00,000
The syntax check asks, did the response parse and match the schema shape?

32
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:01,000
That's necessary.

33
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:03,000
But notice what it doesn't tell you.

34
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:07,000
It says nothing about whether the values are actually correct.

35
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:12,000
A date can be well-typed and still be in the past when your rule says it can't be.

36
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:15,000
The semantic check asks the harder question.

37
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:19,000
Do the values obey your business rules and make sense together?

38
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:23,000
That's the check that catches a plausible but wrong answer, the kind that passes every

39
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:26,000
type check and still breaks your workflow.

40
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:28,000
Syntax proves it's well-formed.

41
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:31,000
Semantics proves it's right. You need both.

42
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:35,000
Let me push on why a single end-of-pipeline check isn't enough.

43
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:40,000
A one-shot check quietly assumes the first generation is either correct or it throws

44
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:42,000
an error you'll catch.

45
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:44,000
Neither is guaranteed.

46
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:46,000
Silent failures are the trap.

47
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:49,000
The call returns success, no exception.

48
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:54,000
But the data is wrong or incomplete, and because nothing fired, that bad data flows

49
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:57,000
straight downstream into the next step.

50
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:02,000
Layer on non-determinism, the same prompt can produce a different answer run-to-run.

51
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:05,000
So a prompt that passed yesterday can fail today.

52
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:08,000
Put those together and the lesson is blunt.

53
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:13,000
Without a reprompt path, bad output sails through, and the pipeline lets wrong data

54
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:15,000
pass undetected.

55
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:19,000
Now the part that catches even careful developers.

56
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:26,000
Accurate outputs constrain generation, but a few edge cases and quirks still deserve a guard. Four

57
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:28,000
to know. First, refusal.

58
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:34,000
If the model declines on safety grounds, stop reason comes back as refusal, and the output

59
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:36,000
may not match your schema at all.

60
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:38,000
Second, max tokens.

61
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:46,000
If generation hits the token limit, stop reason is max tokens, and your object can be truncated mid-structure.

62
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:49,000
Third, enum capitalization.

63
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:57,000
Enum values can differ only in case, so compare them case insensitively rather than with exact equality.

64
00:03:57,000 --> 00:04:00,000
Fourth, property ordering.

65
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:06,000
The API doesn't guarantee any property order, so always read fields by name, never by position.

66
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:09,000
Four cases, all worth a guard.

67
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:14,000
So before you trust a structured payload, do one cheap thing first.

68
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:15,000
Check why the model stopped.

69
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:18,000
Read stop reason before you parse.

70
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:23,000
If it's a refusal, there's no schema valid object waiting for you, so handle it as a

71
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:26,000
refusal, not as malformed data.

72
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:30,000
If it's max tokens, the response may be cut off part way through, and parsing a truncated

73
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:33,000
object gives you garbage.

74
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:38,000
Only a normal completion, an end turn stop, earns your usual parsing path.

75
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:42,000
This one check saves you from a whole category of confusing bugs, where your code tries to

76
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:46,000
read fields out of an answer the model never actually finished writing.

77
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:50,000
Stop reason first, then parse.

78
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:53,000
Let's stack the validation into layers you can implement.

79
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:55,000
The first layer is schema validation.

80
00:04:55,000 --> 00:05:01,000
Confirm the response parses, and every field carries its declared type.

81
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:05,000
That's your syntax gate, and structured outputs handle most of it for you.

82
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:08,000
The second layer is business rule checks.

83
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:12,000
Confirm the values are allowed, consistent, and complete for your domain.

84
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:19,000
All the things a schema can't express, like a total that has to equal the sum of its line items.

85
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:21,000
The third layer is reprompt on failure.

86
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:27,000
When either gate fails, you feed the exact error back so the model corrects itself, closing the loop.

87
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:31,000
Schema, business rules, reprompt.

88
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:35,000
Each layer catches what the one before it can't.

89
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:41,000
Let's make the reprompt itself actually useful, because a lazy retry wastes a call.

90
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:45,000
Give the model everything it needs to fix the specific problem.

91
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:48,000
Include the exact error, not a vague try again.

92
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:53,000
Show it the offending output so it can see what it produced and where it went wrong.

93
00:05:53,000 --> 00:06:00,000
And cap your retries, because if the model fails three times in a row, looping forever won't help.

94
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:03,000
You want it to escalate to a human or a fallback.

95
00:06:03,000 --> 00:06:05,000
One more distinction while you're here.

96
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:09,000
Tell a genuine refusal, apart from a bug.

97
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:12,000
A refusal is the model declining on purpose.

98
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:14,000
A bug is an integration failure.

99
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:17,000
Each needs a different fix.

100
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:19,000
Here's what to take away.

101
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:25,000
Response validation turns one hopeful generation into a loop that checks meaning and reprompts on failure.

102
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:30,000
It matters because structured output still breaks on a refusal or a token limit.

103
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:33,000
Just remember, check stop reason before you parse.

104
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:37,000
And that covers validating and reprompting on failure.

105
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:38,000
I'll see you in the next one.

